Date: Wed, 13 May 2026 14:30:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?iOS=20ARM64EC:=20SEH=20survival=20plumbing=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=E2=80=94=20callret=20bump=20+=20guest-stack=20rethrow?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two coordinated changes that let Thumper survive ~20k caught Mach
exceptions on iOS instead of fatally terminating:

1. CALLRET_STACK_SIZE bumped 4 MB → 16 MB (stopgap, not a principled fix).
   SEH unwinds through translated code on iOS don't pop FEX's callret
   entries because we don't have callret-aware SEH cleanup yet. With
   thousands of caught faults on FMOD's worker thread, the original 4 MB
   filled up. 16 MB buys ~1 M entries — enough to survive normal game
   runtimes with a few hundred caught faults per thread. The principled
   fix is callret-aware SEH unwind, tracked for later.

2. Module.cpp:794 unconditional fatal bail when JIT-code fault arrives
   on the guest stack converted to a "rethrow anyway" path under
   FEX_IOS_HOST. iOS doesn't switch exception delivery to a separate
   emulator stack like Windows-ARM64EC does (no CPU_AREA-driven
   sigaltstack); Mach exception delivery arrives on whatever thread
   stack is current, usually the guest stack. The Windows-only check
   IsEmulatorStackAddress() always fails on iOS, so all JIT-code faults
   were terminating the process. Now we attempt RethrowGuestException
   anyway — same code Windows runs — and on this run that diverted 2
   propagated C0000005s into recoverable guest-SEH dispatch.

Effect on Thumper iOS port: process now survives the FMOD-worker
ARM64EC entry-thunk NULL-deref loop (caused upstream by FMOD running
without a real-time audio output clock). Game runs 13.7 M log lines
with 20 k+ caught Mach exceptions, no NtTerminateProcess. Next blocker
is the audio output clock itself, not exception machinery.

Madeira-commit: 8f0243b30303aaeacb4a7f6d7e96f61b6e4fb316
Rebased: clean
Class: madeira-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-09-24-fex-latest-rebase.md
Offered-upstream: no
---
 .../include/FEXCore/Debug/InternalThreadState.h |  8 +++++++-
 Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp               | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 24 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/FEXCore/include/FEXCore/Debug/InternalThreadState.h b/FEXCore/include/FEXCore/Debug/InternalThreadState.h
index 332169d..a9d1d52 100644
--- a/FEXCore/include/FEXCore/Debug/InternalThreadState.h
+++ b/FEXCore/include/FEXCore/Debug/InternalThreadState.h
@@ -109,7 +109,13 @@ struct alignas(FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE) InternalThreadState : public FEXCo
   ///< Data pointer for exclusive use by the frontend
   void* FrontendPtr;
 
-  static constexpr size_t CALLRET_STACK_SIZE {0x400000};
+  // iOS-Mythic 2026-05-13: bumped 4MB → 16MB. SEH unwinds through translated
+  // code on iOS don't pop FEX callret entries (FEX has no SEH-aware callret
+  // cleanup yet), so caught faults leak entries. With 9700+ caught faults
+  // observed in Thumper FMOD worker, the original 4MB filled up. 16MB buys
+  // ~1M entries — enough to survive normal game runtimes that have a few
+  // hundred per-thread caught faults. Real fix: callret-aware SEH unwind.
+  static constexpr size_t CALLRET_STACK_SIZE {0x1000000};
 
   // The low address of the call-ret stack allocation (not including guard pages)
   void* CallRetStackBase {};
diff --git a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
index 3b859b5..4e97870 100644
--- a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
+++ b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
@@ -800,8 +800,25 @@ bool ResetToConsistentStateImpl(const ThreadCPUArea CPUArea, EXCEPTION_RECORD* E
     LogMan::Msg::DFmt("Rethrowing onto guest stack: {:X}", NativeContext->Sp);
     return true;
   } else {
+#ifdef FEX_IOS_HOST
+    // iOS-Mythic 2026-05-13: iOS doesn't switch exception delivery to a
+    // separate emulator stack like Windows-ARM64EC does (no CPU_AREA-driven
+    // sigaltstack). Mach exception delivery arrives on whatever thread
+    // stack was current, which is usually the guest stack. Attempt rethrow
+    // anyway — same path Windows takes; worst case we crash here instead
+    // of terminating with "Unexpected exception" silently. Better signal
+    // than fatal bail.
+    LogMan::Msg::EFmt("iOS: rethrowing JIT fault onto guest stack despite "
+                       "frame@{:X} not in emulator-stack range ({:X}..{:X})",
+                       (uint64_t)__builtin_frame_address(0),
+                       CPUArea.EmulatorStackLimit(),
+                       CPUArea.EmulatorStackBase());
+    Exception::RethrowGuestException(*Exception, *NativeContext);
+    return true;
+#else
     LogMan::Msg::EFmt("Unexpected exception in JIT code on guest stack");
     return false;
+#endif
   }
 }
 
